---
/**
 * /archive/ — Archive: the browse hub AND the site's keyword search, on one
 * page (axis-chip redesign, 2026-09-09 — supersedes the 2026-09-09
 * search-hub design this file already carried; masthead label reverts to
 * "Archive", route unchanged — see Masthead.astro's intro).
 *
 * THE REDESIGN, IN ONE SENTENCE: the previous round turned the four axes
 * (Year/Genre/Tags, Artists as a separate page) into LINKS, each opening its
 * own listing page. The decision-maker asked for the opposite shape — every
 * axis value as a pressable chip that filters the list IN PLACE, "비슷한
 * 느낌" to the query box that already sits above them — so a click and a
 * typed word now do the exact same thing: fill the box, run the one filter.
 * lib/derive/archive.ts#deriveHubIndex's `haystack` is still the single
 * definition of "matches" (data-s on each row); a chip's `data-axis-value`
 * is nothing more than a pre-written query into that same string.
 *
 * FOUR AXES NOW, NOT THREE-PLUS-A-PRESET: Year · Genre · Artist · Tag. Artist
 * was a standalone page (`/artists/`, 2026-09-07) and is folded back in as a
 * chip axis — its rows come straight from `artistIndex` (name order, same
 * data the old page rendered), so nothing about "which artists exist" is
 * recomputed here. `/artists/{slug}/` ITSELF IS NOT TOUCHED: a review's
 * artist byline still links there ("아티스트 소개 페이지" — a real page with
 * its own intro prose, not a filtered list), only the axis LISTING of every
 * artist moved from its own route into this page's chip row. The two format
 * presets, /archive/reviews/ and /archive/stories/, are UNCHANGED — they are
 * not axes of the archive, they are the two things this magazine publishes
 * (masthead's Reviews/Notes targets), and stay their own full pages.
 *
 * PAGES THAT NO LONGER EXIST, AS OF THIS EDIT: `/archive/{year}/`,
 * `/archive/genre/{bucket}/`, `/archive/tag/{tag}/`, `/artists/` (the list —
 * not `/artists/{slug}/`). Every static link this codebase had to any of
 * them was migrated to a chip or to a `/archive/?q=` link (SpecMeta's
 * Genre/Tags/Release rows) — see 08-impl-notes/frontend.md for the audit.
 *
 * CHIPS ARE BUTTONS, NOT LINKS (WAI-ARIA: an in-page action, not navigation),
 * so with JS OFF they simply sit there — inert, but not broken, because the
 * page under them is already the full, unfiltered archive server-rendered in
 * full (see the ORIGINAL search-hub reasoning below, which still holds:
 * "the hub is the index"). `aria-pressed` carries the selected state — never
 * colour alone (WCAG 1.4.1); enhance.js flips it in the same pass that runs
 * the filter, comparing each chip's own value against the query, so a chip
 * clicked, a value typed by hand, or a value arriving via `?q=` all leave
 * the row of chips in a consistent state.
 *
 * THE ORIGINAL SEARCH-HUB REASONING (2026-09-09, unchanged): this site ships
 * with JS OFF as a first-class mode (browser.flows.spec.ts builds every page
 * with `javaScriptEnabled: false`), and a search box that fetches a JSON
 * index cannot work there. THE HUB IS THE INDEX — every article renders
 * here, server-side, exactly as /archive/reviews/ or the old /artists/
 * already rendered their own lists; the query box and the chips only HIDE
 * rows a value does not match, in place, with the `hidden` attribute — no
 * fetch, no separate index file. With JS off the controls sit inert and
 * every row stays visible, which is to say the page falls back to being
 * exactly what it already was: an archive.
 *
 * THE RESULT COUNT IS HIDDEN AT REST, VISIBLE ONLY WHILE FILTERING
 * (2026-09-09, decision-maker: "SEARCH 입력창 옆의 '1개' — 없애도 될 것
 * 같은데?"). Removing it outright would cost a screen-reader user the one
 * thing `aria-live` gave them — how many rows a query just left standing —
 * so it is not gone, only quiet when there is nothing to report: an empty
 * query matches everything, and "all of them" is not a number worth
 * announcing when the full list is already sitting there, visibly complete.
 * It un-hides the instant a query (typed OR a chip) narrows anything, and
 * with JS off it never un-hides at all — which is correct, not a gap: JS off
 * means nothing is ever filtered, so "how many survived the filter" has no
 * answer to give.
 *
 * THE MAGNIFYING-GLASS IS A `<label>`, NOT A `<button>`, AND SHIPS NO JS AT
 * ALL (2026-09-09, decision-maker: "SEARCH 옆에 '1개' 대신 돋보기 버튼").
 * Clicking OR tapping a `<label for="archive-q">` moves focus to the input
 * it names — a native browser behaviour, not a script — which is exactly
 * "시각 요소 + 포커스 이동" and nothing more: the brief was explicit that
 * typing must keep filtering live (already true, unchanged) and that the
 * icon must not turn this into a "press to search" control. A real
 * `<button>` here would need a click LISTENER to do the same job — one this
 * page does not have to spend any of its byte budget on. It is
 * `aria-hidden` so a screen reader is not handed the same accessible name
 * ("Search") twice from two different labels on one input; the visible
 * "Search" text above the box remains the field's one spoken name.
 */
import ArticleCard from '../../components/ArticleCard.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { bucketLabelMap, ETC_BUCKET_LABEL } from '../../lib/derive/archive.ts';
import { getSiteData } from '../../lib/derive/site-data.ts';

const { archive, hubIndex, data, artistIndex } = getSiteData();

const years = [...archive.by_year.keys()].sort().reverse();

const bucketLabels = bucketLabelMap(data);
const buckets = [...archive.by_bucket.keys()]
  .sort()
  .map((id) => ({ id, label: id === 'etc' ? ETC_BUCKET_LABEL : (bucketLabels.get(id) ?? id) }));

const tagLabels = new Map((data.tags?.tags ?? []).map((t) => [t.slug, t.label]));
const tags = [...archive.by_tag.keys()].sort().map((slug) => ({ slug, label: tagLabels.get(slug) ?? slug }));
---

<Base title="Archive" description="제목·아티스트·연도·장르·태그로 찾는 전체 글." path="/archive/" current="archive">
  <main class="archive">
    <h1 class="page-title">Archive</h1>

    {
      /* R-4, same as every axis below: nothing to search is nothing to
         render — an empty site has no query box and no chip either. */
      hubIndex.length > 0 && (
        <section class="search" aria-label="검색">
          <label for="archive-q" class="axis-title">Search</label>
          <div class="search-row">
            <div class="search-field">
              <input
                id="archive-q"
                type="search"
                class="search-input"
                autocomplete="off"
                spellcheck="false"
                placeholder="제목·아티스트·연도·장르·태그"
                aria-describedby="archive-n"
              />
              {/* Zero-JS focus shortcut — see the component intro. */}
              <label for="archive-q" class="search-icon-btn" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <circle cx="8.5" cy="8.5" r="6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
                  <line x1="13.2" y1="13.2" x2="18" y2="18" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" />
                </svg>
              </label>
            </div>
            {/* Live region: hidden at rest, un-hidden by enhance.js the moment
                a query (typed or a chip) narrows the list — see the intro. */}
            <p id="archive-n" class="search-count tnum" aria-live="polite" hidden>{hubIndex.length}개</p>
          </div>
        </section>
      )
    }

    {
      years.length > 0 && (
        <section class="axis">
          <h2 class="axis-title">Year</h2>
          <ul class="values">
            {years.map((year) => (
              <li>
                <button type="button" class="chip tnum" data-axis-value={year} aria-pressed="false">{year}</button>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      )
    }

    {
      buckets.length > 0 && (
        <section class="axis">
          <h2 class="axis-title">Genre</h2>
          <ul class="values">
            {buckets.map((bucket) => (
              <li>
                <button type="button" class="chip" data-axis-value={bucket.label} aria-pressed="false">{bucket.label}</button>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      )
    }

    {
      /* Artist axis (2026-09-09, folded back in from the retired /artists/
         list page) — same rows, same name order, as a chip row instead of a
         full page of links. */
      artistIndex.length > 0 && (
        <section class="axis">
          <h2 class="axis-title">Artist</h2>
          <ul class="values">
            {artistIndex.map((artist) => (
              <li>
                <button type="button" class="chip" data-axis-value={artist.name} aria-pressed="false">{artist.name}</button>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      )
    }

    {
      /* Tag tab hidden entirely while no tags exist (ui-spec §6). */
      tags.length > 0 && (
        <section class="axis">
          <h2 class="axis-title">Tag</h2>
          <ul class="values">
            {tags.map((tag) => (
              <li>
                <button type="button" class="chip" data-axis-value={tag.label} aria-pressed="false">{tag.label}</button>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      )
    }

    {
      /* The listing itself — every article, server-rendered, mixed types,
         no section heading (2026-09-09: "ALL 부분은 없어도 되지 않을까?" —
         removed; the list needs no label of its own once it is the page's
         only remaining unlabelled content, immediately below four named
         axes). */
      hubIndex.length > 0 && (
        <ul class="all-cards" id="archive-list">
          {hubIndex.map((hub) => (
            <ArticleCard item={hub.item} searchKey={hub.haystack} />
          ))}
        </ul>
      )
    }
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .archive {
    width: min(var(--shell), 100% - 2 * var(--gutter));
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--s-48) 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-32);
  }
  /* TITLE→CONTENT IS THE SITE'S ONE SHARED GAP, --s-24, NOT THIS PAGE'S OWN
     --s-32 — see archive/index.astro's git history (2026-09-08) for the full
     derivation; the trick is pinned to "h1, then the flex column's next
     item", so it survives axes being added/removed/reordered around it. */
  .page-title {
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--s-24) - var(--s-32));
  }
  .search {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-8);
  }
  .search-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s-16);
  }
  .search-field {
    position: relative;
    flex: 1 1 280px;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  /* Square corners (--r-0, matching every other surface here) and native
     appearance reset: Safari/Chromium give type=search its own rounded pill
     chrome and internal padding that no other control on this site carries.
     Right padding clears the icon label sitting inside the field. */
  .search-input {
    appearance: none;
    -webkit-appearance: none;
    width: 100%;
    min-height: 44px;
    /* Right padding matches .search-icon-btn's own 44px box exactly, not a
       spacing-scale token — the two numbers describe the same geometry
       (clearing the icon), the same way .thumb-score's dimensions elsewhere
       on this site are sized to the object they sit on rather than to the
       spacing scale. */
    padding: 0 44px 0 var(--s-12);
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-0);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: inherit;
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
  }
  .search-input::placeholder {
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  /* A native <label>, styled to read as a button — clicking/tapping it moves
     focus to the input via the browser's own for= behaviour, no JS (see the
     component intro). Positioned inside the field's right edge like an
     ordinary search-icon affordance. */
  .search-icon-btn {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    right: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 44px;
    height: 44px;
    color: var(--muted);
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .search-field:focus-within .search-icon-btn {
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }
  .search-count {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .axis-title {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    line-height: 16px;
    letter-spacing: var(--label-ls);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .values {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--s-8) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s-8) var(--s-12);
  }
  /* Every axis value is the SAME button shape now (2026-09-09 — supersedes
     the two-tier bold/muted split this page used to draw between buckets and
     tags, ADR-0007; that split stays everywhere ELSE the two appear, e.g.
     SpecMeta's own Genre/Tags rows — this page alone was asked to unify).
     Bordered rather than filled at rest so a page of forty chips does not
     read as forty solid blocks before a single one is pressed. */
  .chip {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 var(--s-12);
    background: var(--surface-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-0);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-13);
    line-height: 16px;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background var(--t-micro), border-color var(--t-micro), color var(--t-micro);
  }
  .chip:hover {
    border-color: var(--line-strong);
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }
  /* Pressed state: fill + ink swap, never colour alone — aria-pressed is the
     programmatic half (screen readers announce "pressed"), the fill/border
     swap is the visual half. --accent-2 (not --accent): --accent is reserved
     for "whatever the score made" (D2, §2.3) and this is a browsing surface
     with no score in sight, exactly the rule the rest of this page already
     follows for ArticleCard's showScore. */
  .chip[aria-pressed='true'] {
    background: var(--accent-2);
    border-color: var(--accent-2);
    color: var(--on-accent-2);
  }
  /* NO outline-color OVERRIDE HERE, ON PURPOSE (measured, not assumed —
     2026-09-09). The global focus ring sits `outline-offset: 2px` OUTSIDE
     the element's border box (global.css), so on a chip — an ordinary
     inline-flex box with no extra padding around it — the ring lands on the
     PAGE's own background, not on the chip's `--accent-2` fill underneath
     it. `--on-accent-2` is tuned for text painted ON that fill (8.72:1,
     tokens.css) and reads at only ~1.1:1 against the page background the
     ring actually sits over — verified by browser.contrast.spec.ts's own
     ring probe, which is what caught this rather than the pattern below
     being trusted on sight. `.plate`'s matching override (global.css) is
     NOT the same situation: `.plate :focus-visible` targets a DESCENDANT
     focused inside a plate's own padding, where the offset ring can still
     land on the plate's fill — this chip has no such descendant, it is the
     focus target itself. The default mint ring against the page background
     is what every other unpressed chip and every other control on this page
     already uses, and it clears the same 3:1 floor here too. */
  .all-cards {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .archive {
      padding-top: var(--s-32);
    }
  }
</style>
